From 7250373a52ce0e5fe56c793832cf2c427d31dd41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 06:59:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] History: a move made over records on the files and in the
 test store goes by the tombstones read before what is here is listed as well
 as after the records are read, as in a browser (fixes a deletion another copy
 made in between being undone, and an Undo made in between leaving its session
 stored twice)

record_store::here_of, the look at what is here that the move of a store
that moves before it reads makes (Order::MoveThenRead: the files and the
test store), listed the records and tombstones, read the tombstones
once, and then read what the records under names the move does not give
hold. A session another copy deleted after that one read and before its
record was read was neither here nor deleted, and the move stored it
again from the history kept whole: the deletion was undone. One it put
back, by Undo or a restore, after the listing and before that read was
neither listed nor named by a tombstone, and the move stored it beside
the record the Undo put back. here_of now reads the tombstones before
the listing as well as after the texts, takes the keys of both reads,
and by name goes by the later, as read_in's move in a browser does since
2ec6e8e and 0e222e3; a refused read, or a tombstone that cannot be read
just then, refuses the move as the one read did, and nothing is here
only where neither the first read nor the listing finds anything.
de8dd8d gave the files' take-up these reads and left the move made again
as it was. On the files an Undo takes the tombstone away before it
stores the record, so one whose own two steps span this copy's first
read of the tombstones and its listing of the records still leaves the
session neither: that window is the Undo's own, as at a take-up.

For a test to act between the move's reads, the files' hook for another
copy (HistoryFiles::meanwhile) is now done too as Disk::here has read
each folder and as Disk::tombstones has read them, and the test store
gains one, MemoryStore::elsewhere, done as InMem::here and
InMem::tombstones have read. Each new test has another copy make its
change at every such read in turn. Before the fix,
persist::files::tests::a_deletion_another_copy_makes_as_this_one_moves_is_not_undone,
with its worker twin, found the session back, under the move's own name,
with the deletion made at the third read, once the tombstones were read,
and there alone; an_undo_another_copy_makes_as_this_one_moves_leaves_one_record,
with its twin, found it twice with the Undo made at the first or the
second, once the records or the tombstones were listed; and the test
store's tests of the same names failed so at its second read and at its
first. Which reads failed was seen in a run of the four that noted each
read's outcome rather than stop at the first failure. The core's
a_store_that_moves_first_moves_again_over_records_reading_the_tombstones_before_and_after
pins the order, and failed seeing here, tombstones, texts;
a_store_that_moves_first_makes_the_move_and_then_reads_what_it_made
expects the tombstones read before here. The hook's new calls move on by
one the two files tests that count its calls through a take-up's read of
the tombstones (a_take_up_that_cannot_be_made_now_is_still_to_take_up,
an_undo_another_copy_makes_as_this_one_takes_up_leaves_one_record),
which now act at the fourth, the moment they meant; and
the_move_and_the_read_say_how_far_they_have_got looks at the first count
seen, as the move's look at what is here counts nothing.

Ran: cargo fmt --all --check; cargo clippy -D warnings for desktop
(--workspace --all-targets), web (wasm32-unknown-unknown) and
mobile-silent (--all-targets); cargo test -p dust --features desktop
persist, 437 passed, and the whole desktop suite, 1843 passed; cargo
test -p dust --no-default-features --features mobile-silent persist,
437 passed; lifecycle_walk, 10 passed.

